# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Assistant tooling — local only, never committed.
# See PLAN.md §8.1. This is about the KIND of file, not who authored it:
# PLAN.md, docs/**, README.md and the other project docs ARE committed.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CLAUDE.md
CLAUDE.local.md
.claude/
.mcp.json

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Point-in-time working documents — local only.
#
# An audit or review report describes the tree on one day. Its findings belong in the documents
# that own them (THREAT_MODEL, BRANCH_PROTECTION, RELEASE, this file), and once they are moved
# there the report is a second, ageing copy that contradicts them the first time one is updated.
# The 2026-08-15 audit was committed in `29c5114` and is untracked again from 2026-08-16; it stays
# in history because it carries nothing sensitive, and it is not deleted from disk.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
AUDIT_AND_ACTION_PLAN.md
AUDIT*.md

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Secrets and signing keys — a leak here is a publishing-credential compromise.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.pem
*.crx
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# The JSON the Google Cloud console hands you when you create an OAuth client. For the Chrome
# Extension client type it carries no secret — only the client id, which also lives in the shipped
# manifest and is public by design (RELEASE §5.3). It is ignored anyway: the id belongs in
# `.env.local`, the file is named after the project it came from, and the day someone downloads the
# *web application* flavour of it by mistake, that one does carry a secret.
client_secret_*.json

